## Chapter 14: The Hierarchy on Display
The Joint Sector Defense Exercise was not designed to be fair. It was designed to be broadcasted.
By 0700 hours, three massive military transit hover-transports had deposited over three hundred students into the staging plaza of **Sector 4’s Ruined Residential Grid**—a massive, walled-off five-square-kilometer zone of shattered concrete high-rises, collapsed sky-bridges, and encroaching Colony Zone flora.
Above the staging ground, high-altitude camera drones hovered like silver wasps, beaming live feeds back to corporate sponsors, guild recruiters, and city official stations across the Upper Core.
Kaelen stood near the back of the Anvil Ridge staging pen, adjusting the strap of his heavy scrap-pack. He wore his standard frayed uniform, his boots coated in a layer of old mud. Underneath his loose sleeves and pants, his custom-forged high-density bracers and insoles sat flush against his skin, invisible to the sweeping cameras.
Beside him, Renna adjusted her tower shield, her expression grim as she surveyed the staging plaza. Tobias was nervously checking the string on his heavy crossbow for the tenth time, while Mina twirled her daggers with quiet focus.
"Look at those bastards," Tobias muttered, nodding toward the northern quadrant of the plaza.
The students from **Gideon Prep** occupied a pristine, covered pavilion. They wore matching, custom-tailored silver-and-blue tactical coats embedded with glowing blue aetheric runes. Their weapons—enchanted broadswords, high-grade elemental staff units, and composite bows—shimmered with rich, expensive mana fields. Servants in corporate uniforms passed out insulated thermoses of high-grade essence-replenishment tonics.
In the center of the plaza stood the cadets from **Ironclad Academy**.
They didn't have a pavilion. They didn't need one. Thirty cadets stood in rigid military formation, clad in matte-black kinetic plate armor. At their head stood **Leon Thorne**.
Leon was towering, his short-cropped dark hair framing a sharp, cold face. He wore no helmet, and a massive, six-foot kinetic warhammer was strapped to his back like a piece of industrial machinery. The air around him subtly distorted from the passive kinetic pressure radiating from his Tier-A class.
A harsh, synthesized chime echoed over the plaza's loudspeaker system, cutting through the murmurs of the crowd.
An officer from the City Defense Bureau stepped onto the elevated podium, his voice amplified across the arena.
"Cadets! Welcome to the Joint Sector Assessment," the officer boomed. "The exercise parameters are simple. Sector 4 contains three threat zones: Sector-C (Outer Perimeter), Sector-B (Commercial Ruins), and Sector-A (Central Breach Core)."
The holographic map above the podium flashed with color-coded districts.
"Your objective is to collect **Target Tags** from roaming monsters and secure designated control nodes," the officer continued. "Sector-C monsters yield Tier-E tags (1 Point). Sector-B yields Tier-D tags (5 Points). Sector-A yields Tier-D Elite and Tier-C tags (20 Points). The top three squads with the highest score at the end of four hours receive direct scouting offers from Tier-1 Guilds."
Leon Thorne didn't even look at the map. He turned to his squad, his voice carrying clearly across the quiet plaza.
"We move straight to Sector-A," Leon ordered coldly. "Ignore the outer perimeter. If any auxiliary squads get in our way or draw stragglers into our clearing zone, sweep them aside. We aren't here to play babysitter for trash."
Several Gideon Prep leaders chuckled, while the Anvil Ridge students clenched their fists in silent anger.
"Now, sector assignments!" the officer announced. "Gideon Prep—Sector-B West. Ironclad Academy—Sector-A Core. Anvil Ridge Vocational—Sector-C East Perimeter."
"Surprise, surprise," Renna spat quietly, her jaw tight. "They put us in the deepest, most collapsed quadrant of the outer zone where the scrap yield is lowest."
"It's fine, Renna," Kaelen said softly, keeping his voice meek as he adjusted his pack. "Fewer people means less trouble. We just do our perimeter loop, set the traps, and collect what we need."
Renna looked at Kaelen, her tight expression softening slightly. "Yeah. You're right, Kaelen. We play it smart. We stick together, hold our line, and let Ironclad and Gideon exhaust themselves in the center."
*BZZZZZZT!*
The massive iron blast gates surrounding the staging plaza groaned as they began to lift, revealing the overgrown, fog-covered ruins of Sector 4.
"Exercise begins NOW!" the officer shouted.
Instantly, Ironclad’s squad surged forward in a disciplined, heavy sprint, their kinetic boots cracking the asphalt as they charged straight toward the high-value Sector-A core. Gideon Prep followed close behind, their magical shields flickering into blue light as they spread out toward the commercial sector.
Renna raised her tower shield. "Anvil Ridge Squad 4! Move out to Sector-C East!"
As Kaelen jogged behind Renna, Tobias, and Mina into the shadow of the crumbling high-rises, his **[Hive-Mind Synapse]** flared quietly behind his eyes.
His expanded perception swept over the surrounding fog, reading the faint, shifting energy signatures hidden deep within the ruins.
Ironclad thought they were charging into a controlled testing ground. But deep in the damp subterranean sub-basements beneath Sector-C, Kaelen felt a dark, violent pulse vibrating through the concrete bedrock—a pulse far too dense for a simple mock-breach exercise.
A faint, cold smile flickered beneath Kaelen's fabric mask before vanishing back into his timid expression.
*Let the show begin.*
